/** Generated from the drums textbook. Re-run tools/import-drums-textbook.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"drums-level-10","number":10,"title":"Survey of Contemporary Rhythms","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the drums textbook","summary":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes While Dubstep (~140 BPM Half-Time) and Drum & Bass (~174 BPM Breakbeats) are the primary rhythmic engines of this curriculum, complete electronic drum literacy requires understanding contemporary four-on","modules":[{"id":"drums-m10-1","code":"10.1","title":"Contemporary Electronic Rhythms — Techno & Four-on-the-Floor Cousins","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes While Dubstep (~140 BPM Half-Time) and Drum & Bass (~174 BPM Breakbeats) are the primary rhythmic engines of this curriculum, complete electronic drum literacy requires understanding contemporary four-on","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-10-1","title":"Section 10.1 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While Dubstep (~140 BPM Half-Time) and Drum &amp; Bass (~174 BPM Breakbeats) are the primary rhythmic engines of this curriculum, complete electronic drum literacy requires understanding contemporary four-on-the-floor genres. <strong>Techno</strong> (128.00–135.00 BPM) represents the hypnotic, industrial extreme of the 4/4 metric grid. This module explores machine-driven four-on-the-floor patterns, the synthesis of the <strong>Techno Reverb Rumble Kick</strong>, and the aesthetic function of 4/4 time in electronic club culture."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Analyze</strong> the aesthetic and functional differences between House (124–128 BPM) and Techno (128–135 BPM).","<strong>Synthesize</strong> a sub-frequency Techno Reverb Rumble Kick inside an Ableton Live 12 Drum Rack.","<strong>Program</strong> hypnotic, minimalist offbeat percussion that avoids high-frequency clutter.","<strong>Compare</strong> the physical club impact of continuous 4/4 kick pulses against syncopated breakbeats and half-time drops."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-1-1","title":"10.1.1: Four-on-the-Floor Recall & Tempo Escalation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.1: Four-on-the-Floor Recall & Tempo Escalation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Section 3.1, we established foundational 4/4 literacy through classic House (125.00 BPM). In contemporary Techno:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Tempo Standard:</strong> Accelerates to <strong>128.00–135.00 BPM</strong> (with Peak-Time / Raw Techno pushing 132–138 BPM).","<strong>Metric Anchor:</strong> Kick triggers relentlessly on Steps <strong>1, 5, 9, 13</strong>.","<strong>Aesthetic Shift:</strong> Removes funky vocal chops, swing shuffles, and open claps in favor of <strong>cold, industrial, hypnotic repetition</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-1-2","title":"10.1.2: The Techno Reverb Rumble Kick Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.2: The Techno Reverb Rumble Kick Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In modern Techno (Drumcode, Afterlife, Ostgut Ton), the low-end sub-bass is not generated by an external synthesizer; it is generated by <strong>processing the kick drum's own transient through a distorted, band-passed reverb</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 TECHNO REVERB RUMBLE KICK PARALLEL SIGNAL CHAIN\n==================================================================================================\nDRUM RACK PAD C1 (KICK DIRECT)\n       |\n       +---&gt; [ BRANCH A: TOP PUNCH (Dry) ]\n       |     • DS Kick / 909 Kick (Tune: 55 Hz, Click: 85%)\n       |     • EQ Eight: High-pass @ 40 Hz (Surgically clean transient punch)\n       |\n       +---&gt; [ BRANCH B: THE REVERB RUMBLE (Wet Sub Bed) ]\n             • Hybrid Reverb: Algorithm [ Dark Cave ] / Decay: 1.2 sec / 100% Wet\n             • Saturator: Mode [ Hard Curve ] / Drive: +8.0 dB (Distorts the reverb tail)\n             • Auto Filter: Low-Pass 24 dB @ 90 Hz (Isolates pure sub-bass mud)\n             • Utility: Bass Mono Engaged @ 120 Hz\n             • Live Compressor: Sidechained to Branch A (Ducks rumble during the kick attack!)\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Acoustic Result:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Every kick hit generates an instantaneous, perfectly phase-aligned sub-bass wave that rolls between downbeats (100–250 ms after the strike), creating a relentless, driving low-end pulse without requiring a separate bassline."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-1-3","title":"10.1.3: What Four-on-the-Floor is For","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.3: What Four-on-the-Floor is For","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 THE THREE METRIC PILLARS OF ELECTRONIC DANCE MUSIC\n==================================================================================================\nMETRIC CLASS       TEMPO STANDARD    PERCEIVED ENERGY         CLUB DANCING FUNCTION\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. Four-on-the-Floor 125 – 135 BPM   Relentless, hypnotic, marching Continuous physical trance &amp; stamina\n2. Half-Time (Dubstep) 140.00 BPM    Heavy, massive, head-nodding  Drop tension &amp; sub-bass physical shock\n3. Breakbeats (DnB) 174 – 175 BPM    High-velocity, aerodynamic   High-speed kinetic kinetic propulsion\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 10.1: Techno Rumble Kick Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In Ableton Live 12, set tempo to <code>132.00 BPM</code>.","Load an empty Drum Rack on Track 1. On Pad C1, group two chains (Branch A: Dry 909 Kick; Branch B: Reverb Rumble Chain).","On Branch B, insert <strong>Hybrid Reverb</strong> (100% Wet) → <strong>Saturator</strong> (+6 dB Drive) → <strong>Auto Filter</strong> (Low-pass @ 85 Hz) → <strong>Compressor</strong> (Sidechained to Branch A).","Program kicks on Steps 1, 5, 9, 13.","Add an offbeat closed hi-hat on Steps 3, 7, 11, 15.","Verify the dark, driving, hypnotic low-end rumble."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>How is a Techno Reverb Rumble Kick synthesized inside Ableton Live?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> By routing the kick drum into a parallel chain featuring a 100% wet reverb, distorting the reverb tail with Saturator, filtering it with a steep low-pass filter below 90 Hz, and sidechain-compressing the rumble to the dry kick transient.","<strong>What is the standard tempo band for contemporary Peak-Time / Industrial Techno?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>128.00 to 135.00 BPM</strong>.","<strong>Why is sidechain compression applied to the Reverb Rumble chain?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> To duck the sub-frequency reverb tail during the initial kick strike (0–30 ms), preventing phase cancellation and preserving transient punch."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m10-2","code":"10.2","title":"Contemporary Electronic Rhythms — Trap & Half-Time Hi-Hat Rolls","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-10-2","title":"Section 10.2 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Trap</strong> (originating in Atlanta hip-hop and evolving into a dominant global production framework) shares the half-time metric clock of Dubstep while utilizing a distinct acoustic vocabulary: tuned, sliding <strong>808 sub-basslines</strong>, ultra-tight <strong>TR-808 snares and claps</strong>, and frantic, polyrhythmic <strong>hi-hat ratchets</strong> modulating between 16th, 32nd, and triplet subdivisions. This module deconstructs Trap rhythm programming using native Ableton Live 12 tools."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Program</strong> Trap half-time skeletons across the 140.00–160.00 BPM tempo band.","<strong>Design</strong> complex, pitch-modulated 32nd-note and triplet hi-hat rolls using <strong>DS HH</strong> and <strong>Drum Sampler</strong>.","<strong>Integrate</strong> acoustic punch kicks with chromatic, sliding 808 sub-bass notes.","<strong>Contrast</strong> the acoustic profiles and roll densities of Trap vs. Dubstep."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-2-1","title":"10.2.1: The Trap Metric Clock: 140–160 BPM Half-Time","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.1: The Trap Metric Clock: 140–160 BPM Half-Time","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           THE TRAP 16-STEP MATRIX (150 BPM HALF-TIME)\n==================================================================================================\nDAW Master Clock:       [ 140.00 – 160.00 BPM ] (Standard Default: 150.00 BPM)\nStep Index (16ths):     01  02  03  04  05  06  07  08  09  10  11  12  13  14  15  16\nQuarter Note Beats:    [BEAT 1]        [BEAT 2]        [BEAT 3]        [BEAT 4]\nTrap Punch Kick:       [K]  .   .   .   .   .   .  [K]  .   .   .   .   .  [K]  .   .\nTrap Snare / Clap:      .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .  [S]  .   .   .   .   .   .   .\n808 Sub-Bass Note:     [F1==========================================================]\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Snare Placement:</strong> Strictly anchored to <strong>Beat 3 (Step 9)</strong>, establishing the half-time drop identity.","<strong>Kick Syncopation:</strong> Kicks strike sparsely on Beat 1, with syncopated ghost kicks landing on offbeats (Step 8 or Step 14) to push into the next measure."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-2-2","title":"10.2.2: Hi-Hat Rolls on Stock Devices","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.2: Hi-Hat Rolls on Stock Devices","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The defining characteristic of Trap is the rapidly modulating <strong>Hi-Hat Roll</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 TRAP HI-HAT SUBDIVISION MODULATION (1 BAR)\n==================================================================================================\nBeat 1 (Steps 1-4)   : Straight 1/16th Notes (Vel: 100, 65, 90, 60)\nBeat 2 (Steps 5-8)   : 1/32nd-Note Ratchet Roll (8 rapid hits; decaying velocity 115 -&gt; 50)\nBeat 3 (Steps 9-12)  : Straight 1/16th Notes\nBeat 4 (Steps 13-16) : 1/24th-Note Triplet Roll (1/16T Triplet Grid; Pitch swept down -5 semitones)\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Implementing Pitch-Swept Rolls in Live 12:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["In the MIDI Note Editor, select the 32nd-note hi-hat burst.","Open the <strong>Pitch Envelope</strong> or <strong>Pitch Expression</strong> lane.","Draw a downward linear ramp from +7 st to 0 st. The hi-hat accelerates downwards in pitch during the roll, creating classic Trap bounce."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-2-3","title":"10.2.3: Kick and 808 Interplay","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.3: Kick and 808 Interplay","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                      TRAP LOW-END INTERACTION SCHEME                    |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ TOP KICK (Drum Sampler) ] ---&gt; High-Pass @ 90 Hz / Ultra-Punch Click  |\n|                                   Strikes on Step 1 and Step 11          |\n|                                         | (Sidechain Trigger)           |\n|                                         v                               |\n|  [ 808 SUB BASS (Simpler) ]  ---&gt; Tuned to Song Key (e.g. F1 = 43.65 Hz)|\n|                                   Glides between F1, G#1, and D#1       |\n|                                   Sidechained: Ducks 2 dB during Kick   |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-2-4","title":"10.2.4: Trap vs. Dubstep: Rhythmic Contrasts","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.4: Trap vs. Dubstep: Rhythmic Contrasts","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           TRAP vs. DUBSTEP COMPARATIVE MATRIX\n==================================================================================================\nCRITERIA               TRAP PRODUCTION                   DUBSTEP PRODUCTION\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTempo Band             140.00 – 160.00 BPM (Fast)        140.00 BPM Locked\nSnare Timbre           Ultra-dry 808 snare / short clap  Massive 200 Hz composite + Dub Reverb\nHi-Hat Behavior        Frenetic 32nd / Triplet rolls     Sparse 8th-note or swung offbeats\nSub-Bass Source        Pitched chromatic 808 with glide  FM synth growls, Reese basses, pure sine\nSpatial Focus          Bone-dry transients; stereo hats  Cavernous room reverb &amp; tape delay sends\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 10.2: Trap Drum Construction","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Ableton Live 12 tempo to <code>150.00 BPM</code>.","Load <strong>DS HH</strong> on Pad F#1 (Mode: 808, Tone: 12 kHz).","Program a 2-bar hi-hat pattern:","Bar 1 Beat 1 &amp; 3: Straight 16ths.","Bar 1 Beat 2: 32nd-note ratchet roll.","Bar 2 Beat 4: 1/16T triplet roll with downward pitch sweep.","Layer a short punch kick on Step 1 and an 808 snare on Step 9.","On Track 2, program a chromatic 808 sub-bassline in Simpler with 50 ms glide."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What is the standard tempo band for modern Trap music?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>140.00 to 160.00 BPM</strong> (most commonly 145–155 BPM).","<strong>How are pitch-swept hi-hat rolls constructed in Ableton Live 12?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> By programming rapid 32nd or triplet notes in Clip View and drawing an automation envelope in the MIDI Pitch Expression / Pitch Envelope lane across the roll.","<strong>What is the primary difference in snare processing between Trap and Dubstep?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Trap snares are ultra-dry, short, and synthetic (808-style), whereas Dubstep snares are heavy, layered composites with massive auxiliary reverb and delay sends."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m10-3","code":"10.3","title":"Contemporary Electronic Rhythms — Boom-Bap, Lo-Fi & Dub Reggae Lineages","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Modern electronic bass music did not emerge in a vacuum; its acoustic philosophy, swing mechanics, and spatial production techniques descend directly from Hip-Hop (Boom-Bap, Lo-Fi) and Jamaican Dub Regga","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-10-3","title":"Section 10.3 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Modern electronic bass music did not emerge in a vacuum; its acoustic philosophy, swing mechanics, and spatial production techniques descend directly from <strong>Hip-Hop (Boom-Bap, Lo-Fi)</strong> and <strong>Jamaican Dub Reggae</strong>. This survey module examines the historical lineage of drum sampling, MPC swing ratios (58–62%), tape saturation, and the auxiliary delay feedback techniques that formed the foundation of UK Bass culture."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Program</strong> classic Boom-Bap drum rhythms (85.00–96.00 BPM) featuring MPC swing and 2+4 snare backbeats.","<strong>Apply</strong> vintage Lo-Fi processing: bit-reduction, vinyl crackle, band-pass filtering, and micro-timing drift (60.00–90.00 BPM).","<strong>Construct</strong> tight, programmed electronic R&amp;B/Pop and programmed electronic rock backbeats.","<strong>Explain</strong> how Jamaican Dub Reggae mixing techniques (auxiliary spring reverbs, tape delays, bass drops) directly shaped modern Dubstep and Drum &amp; Bass."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-3-1","title":"10.3.1 & 10.3.2: Boom-Bap: Kick \"Boom,\" Snare \"Bap\"","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.1 & 10.3.2: Boom-Bap: Kick \"Boom,\" Snare \"Bap\"","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pioneered in 1990s Golden Era hip-hop (DJ Premier, Pete Rock, J Dilla, RZA) on the Akai MPC60 and E-mu SP-1200:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 THE BOOM-BAP 16-STEP MATRIX (90.00 BPM)\n==================================================================================================\nDAW Master Clock:       [ 85.00 – 96.00 BPM ] (Standard Default: 90.00 BPM)\nStep Index (16ths):     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMetric Count:           1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nAcoustic Kick:         [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .\nAcoustic Snare:         .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .\nSwung Closed Hat:      [H]    .    [H]    .    [H]    .    [H]    .    [H]    .    [H]    .    [H]    .    [H]    .\nMPC 16-Swing Applied:   0 ms       +12ms        0 ms       +12ms        0 ms       +12ms        0 ms       +12ms\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Kick (\"Boom\"):</strong> Warm, rounded vinyl kick with natural acoustic decay.","<strong>Snare (\"Bap\"):</strong> Crisp acoustic snare cracked on <strong>Beats 2 and 4 (Steps 5 and 13)</strong>.","<strong>Swing:</strong> <strong>MPC 16-Swing 60%</strong> applied to delay the upbeat sixteenth notes."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-3-3","title":"10.3.3: Lo-Fi Hip-Hop: Soft Transients and Vinyl Flutter","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.3: Lo-Fi Hip-Hop: Soft Transients and Vinyl Flutter","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Lo-Fi production (60.00–90.00 BPM):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Dynamic Filtering:</strong> High-pass @ 100 Hz and low-pass @ 6.5 kHz using <strong>EQ Eight</strong> to simulate vintage transistor radio playback.","<strong>Saturation &amp; Bit Reduction:</strong> Insert <strong>Redux</strong> in Live 12 (Bit Depth: 12-bit, Downsample: 2x) followed by <strong>Saturator</strong> (Warm Tube mode).","<strong>Timing:</strong> Non-quantized, live finger-drummed hits with natural human micro-timing drag (± 15 ms)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-3-4","title":"10.3.4: Programmed R&B/Pop Backbeats","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.4: Programmed R&B/Pop Backbeats","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Contemporary Pop and R&amp;B rhythm tracks combine synthetic 808/909 foundations with acoustic percussion:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Snare / Clap:</strong> Layered on Beats 2 and 4 with micro-delayed stereo claps (+10 ms).","<strong>Hi-Hats:</strong> Straight 16ths with surgical velocity automation; occasional 32nd-note trap fills preceding phrase boundaries."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-3-5","title":"10.3.5: Programmed Electronic Rock Backbeats","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.5: Programmed Electronic Rock Backbeats","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Studio Boundary:</em> Acoustic kit recording is barred; we synthesize programmed electronic rock:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Kick:</strong> Punchy four-on-the-floor or 1+3 rock pattern using <strong>DS Kick</strong> (Tune: 65 Hz, Click: 90%).","<strong>Snare:</strong> Heavy <strong>DS Snare</strong> cracked on Beats 2 and 4.","<strong>Hi-Hats:</strong> Driving eighth notes on closed hats (1/8) with open hat accents on the offbeat \"&amp;\"."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l10-3-6","title":"10.3.6: Jamaican Dub Reggae: The Lineage of UK Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.6: Jamaican Dub Reggae: The Lineage of UK Bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Modern Dubstep and Drum &amp; Bass are the direct digital descendants of 1970s Jamaican Dub mixers (King Tubby, Lee \"Scratch\" Perry, Scientist):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  THE DUB REGGAE TO UK BASS LINEAGE                      |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ 1970s DUB REGGAE (King Tubby) ]                                      |\n|  • Mute drums dynamically on analog console faders                      |\n|  • Send isolated snare hits into infinite spring reverb &amp; Roland Space Echo|\n|  • Deep electric bass fundamental dominates mix                         |\n|                               |                                         |\n|                               v (Evolution through UK Sound Systems)   |\n|  [ MODERN DUBSTEP &amp; DNB (2020s) ]                                       |\n|  • Subtractive arrangement (Muting drums to create the \"Drop\")          |\n|  • Auxiliary Dub Delay throws on snare turnarounds                      |\n|  • Mono sub-bass physical sound-system culture                          |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 10.3: Boom-Bap & Lo-Fi Construction","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Ableton Live 12 tempo to <code>90.00 BPM</code>.","Load acoustic vinyl drum samples into Drum Rack.","Apply <code>Swing 16-60.agr</code> from the Groove Pool with <strong>Timing: 80%</strong> and <strong>Velocity: 50%</strong>.","Insert <strong>Redux</strong> (12-bit mode) and <strong>EQ Eight</strong> (Low-pass @ 7.5 kHz) on the drum group.","Program a classic 2-bar Boom-Bap groove (Kick 1 &amp; 10; Snare 5 &amp; 13).","Verify the warm, unhurried, humanized vinyl swing."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What is the standard tempo band for classic 1990s Boom-Bap hip-hop?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>85.00 to 96.00 BPM</strong> (with 90.00 BPM as the primary default).","<strong>What iconic drum machine introduced the classic 16-Swing 60% template used in Golden Era hip-hop?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> The <strong>Akai MPC60</strong> (designed by Roger Linn).","<strong>How did 1970s Jamaican Dub Reggae mixing techniques influence modern Dubstep drum production?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Dub Reggae pioneered subtractive mixing (muting drums to feature the bass), cavernous auxiliary tape delay throws on isolated snare hits, and sound-system low-end prioritization."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]}]};
